  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Resource Development / Establish Accounts — register attacker-controlled OAuth application", "technique": "T1550.001", "what_happened": "At 2026-05-13 10:02:14 CEST, Wazuh's Azure AD / Entra ID AuditLogs module logged an 'Add application' followed by an 'Add service principal' activity in tenant corp.onmicrosoft.com. The newly registered application is named 'MailSyncPro' — a deliberate typosquat of Microsoft's legitimate sync tooling — and was registered with NO verified publisher (publisher.verifiedPublisherDomain = null) and a redirect URI pointing to an attacker-controlled domain (https://mailsync-pro.10.108.57.184.attacker.io/callback). The app's manifest requests the Microsoft Graph resource (resourceAppId 00000003-0000-0000-c000-000000000000) with a mix of delegated Scope grants and application Role grants covering Mail.ReadWrite, Directory.Read.All, Files.Read.All and User.ReadBasic.All. Wazuh fired custom rule 99202 ('Azure AD: Unverified publisher app registered'). The unverified-publisher + attacker-domain-redirect-URI combination is PDF detection idea A — an unvetted external app entering the tenant outside the Microsoft marketplace.", "chain_reason": "rule.groups contained 'azure' and rule.description matched 'Add application' / 'service principal' / 'unverified publisher' with rule.id 99202 and rule.mitre.id T1550.001. The univariate signature: a brand-new application object plus service principal created in the tenant with verifiedPublisherDomain null and a redirect URI on a non-corporate external domain.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T10:02:14+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Privilege Escalation / Use Alternate Authentication Material — high-privilege delegated consent", "technique": "T1550.001", "what_happened": "At 10:05:30 CEST (~3 minutes after the app registration), the AuditLogs module logged a 'Consent to application' activity for the MailSyncPro service principal. The initiating user was employee07@corp.onmicrosoft.com — a NON-admin account — and the consent additionalDetails recorded consentType=UserConsent and isAdminConsent=false. The modifiedProperties ConsentAction.Permissions value enumerated the granted scope set: 'Mail.ReadWrite Directory.Read.All Files.Read.All User.ReadBasic.All'. This is the illicit-consent core event: a regular employee, almost certainly following a phishing link to an attacker-hosted consent page, approved a dangerous high-privilege Graph permission bundle for an unverified app — bypassing the MFA-gated admin-consent workflow entirely because user consent requires no IT approval. Wazuh fired rule 99200 ('Azure AD: Risky OAuth app consent granted') and rule 99201 ('Azure AD: High-priv delegated permissions'). This is PDF detection idea B — Mail.ReadWrite + Directory.Read.All + Files.Read.All appearing together in a single UserConsent event.", "chain_reason": "rule.groups contained 'azure' and rule.description matched 'Consent to application' / 'high-privilege delegated permissions' with rule.id 99200 and 99201 and rule.mitre.id T1550.001. The consent event references the same MailSyncPro app object created in step 1, the consentType is UserConsent (non-admin), and the scope bundle includes the high-risk Mail.ReadWrite + Directory.Read.All + Files.Read.All triad.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T10:05:30+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Collection / Use Alternate Authentication Material — application access token used against Graph", "technique": "T1550.001", "what_happened": "From 10:08:40 to 10:12:48 CEST, the MailSyncPro service principal acquired an access token via the authorization_code grant and immediately used it to drive Microsoft Graph calls: a GetMessages operation with topParam=999 and selectParam=* (bulk mailbox enumeration — read every message field for up to 999 messages, the canonical mail-exfiltration access pattern) followed by a ListUsers operation (tenant-wide directory reconnaissance enabled by the Directory.Read.All grant). The SignIn for the token was non-interactive (isInteractive=false) and the caller IP recorded against both the token issuance and the Graph activity was 10.108.57.184 — the same attacker host that registered the app and matches the app's attacker-controlled redirect URI. Wazuh fired rule 99205 ('Azure AD: New OAuth app bulk mailbox read via Graph token'). This is the post-consent data-access stage: the illicit-consent app is now actively using its token to enumerate mail and the tenant directory.", "chain_reason": "rule.groups contained 'azure' and rule.description matched 'OAuth app' / 'Graph token' / 'bulk mailbox read' with rule.id 99205 and rule.mitre.id T1550.001. The Graph activity references the same MailSyncPro app, the token sign-in is non-interactive, and the GetMessages topParam=999 selectParam=* pattern plus the ListUsers directory enumeration are the canonical token-driven collection signature immediately following an illicit consent grant.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T10:08:40+0200"}
  ],
